-- Object descriptions; apply after 001 and 002. Safe to reapply.
BEGIN;
COMMENT ON SCHEMA kodiak IS 'What: Namespace containing the application''s database objects. Why: Keeps application objects together and separate from other schemas. How: Qualify queries with kodiak or configure the API search path; assign privileges through a restricted database login.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.countries IS 'What: Country directory with a unique two-letter code and name. Why: Prevents inconsistent country text across companies and ports. How: Admin maintains entries; company, state and port forms select a country ID. Deactivate obsolete entries.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.states IS 'What: States or emirates belonging to countries. Why: Supports country-dependent location choices. How: Filter by countryid; code is unique within a country. A port''s optional state must belong to its country.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.ports IS 'What: Port directory with country, optional state, city and timezone. Why: Gives bookings and tracking consistent origin, destination and event locations. How: Admin maintains ports; API validates timezone names and uses them for display. Timestamps themselves are stored as timestamptz.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.consignees IS 'What: Consignee companies and business contact details. Why: Provides the organization that oversees linked shippers in the agreed business model. How: Admin creates companies and their users; API scopes historical bookings by bookings.consigneeid, not merely the shipper''s current relationship.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.shippers IS 'What: Shipper companies, current consignee and onboarding approval state. Why: Separates a shipping business from its individual login accounts. How: Public signup creates a pending company; admin assigns a consignee and approves it. Approval requires a consignee; API checks active status and blacklists before booking.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.roles IS 'What: Three business role definitions: ADMIN, CONSIGNEE and SHIPPER. Why: Provides consistent role identifiers without repeating role text on users. How: Seed once; users reference roleid. Keep role codes immutable through runtime permissions; API implements each role''s permissions.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.users IS 'What: Named login accounts with role, organization, password hash and access state. Why: Allows multiple accountable users per company and independent login suspension. How: Admin users have no external organization; external users reference exactly their role''s organization. API handles password verification, lockout and session invalidation; nullable passwordhash must not permit password login.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.container_types IS 'What: Container size/type options and refrigeration flag. Why: Standardizes requested and allocated equipment. How: Booking items and physical containers reference this directory; API checks that allocated equipment satisfies the request.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.tracking_statuses IS 'What: Physical tracking milestones with display order and terminal flag. Why: Keeps progress labels consistent and separate from booking approval and holds. How: Tracking events reference statuses; displayorder helps presentation but does not enforce transition order. API validates transitions and terminal closure.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.document_types IS 'What: Categories such as bill of lading, invoice and delivery proof. Why: Makes attachments searchable and understandable. How: Upload forms select documenttypeid; API enforces any required-document checklist.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.restriction_reasons IS 'What: Shared hold and blacklist reason categories. Why: Makes restrictions consistent and reportable without losing detailed explanations. How: Admin selects a category and adds the specific internal/public explanation to a hold or blacklist entry.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.containers IS 'What: Physical container inventory with unique normalized number and equipment type. Why: Allows the same physical container to be used for multiple journeys without losing history. How: Store uppercase compact numbers; SQL checks format, API checks the check digit. Allocate to an open journey; blacklist through blacklists, not a progress status.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.shipper_reviews IS 'What: Append-only onboarding decisions with reviewer and review time. Why: Explains how and why a shipper was approved, rejected or asked for changes. How: Admin API inserts a decision and updates shippers.approvalstatus in one transaction; previous reviews remain unchanged.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.shipper_consignee_history IS 'What: Append-only old/new consignee assignment records with reason and effective time. Why: Preserves company relationship history after transfers. How: Update shippers.consigneeid and insert history atomically. A null previousconsigneeid represents initial assignment. This records a change; it is not a scheduler for future transfers.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.bookings IS 'What: Shipping request header with parties, route, cargo and review status. Why: Groups the customer''s request separately from physical container movement. How: Shipper or admin creates a draft. API derives consignee on submission, freezes submitted party assignments and validates approval/blacklist rules. A pending unassigned shipper cannot create this header because consigneeid is required.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.booking_items IS 'What: Requested container type and quantity, with optional aggregate cargo measurements. Why: Lets one booking request several containers and different equipment types. How: Add one line per requested group; weights, volumes and package counts are totals for that line, not automatically per container. API ensures allocated journeys do not exceed quantity.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.booking_reviews IS 'What: Append-only booking status decisions and reasons. Why: Preserves the review trail rather than retaining only the latest status. How: Insert the review and update bookings.status in one transaction. SQL checks status vocabulary; API checks permitted transitions.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.container_journeys IS 'What: One container movement fulfilling a booking item, with schedule and closure outcome. Why: Gives each movement its own lifecycle, even when containers are reused or a booking has several containers. How: Create before allocation if necessary; containerid may initially be null. One physical container has at most one open journey. Set endedon and outcome together on closure/cancellation; API also writes the terminal event.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.journey_legs IS 'What: Ordered port-to-port sections of a journey with vessel and planned/actual dates. Why: Supports transshipment and intermediate stops without overwriting the original route. How: Number legs from one within a journey. API validates route continuity and overall route consistency; actual times are entered manually.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.tracking_events IS 'What: Append-only manual status observations with occurrence time, location, source and notes. Why: Builds an accountable timeline and distinguishes event time from data-entry time. How: Insert observations; correct mistakes with a new event referencing supersedeseventid and a reason. Replacements must belong to the same journey. Hide internalnotes from external users.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.journey_holds IS 'What: Independent operational holds with reasons, review dates and release details. Why: Keeps restrictions separate from physical progress and permits several simultaneous holds. How: Insert each hold separately; null releasedon means open. Release by setting releasedon, releasedby and releasereason together. API blocks closure while any hold is open.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.blacklists IS 'What: Restrictions targeting either one shipper or one physical container. Why: Blocks future activity while preserving ongoing shipment history. How: Exactly one target is required. Insert a restriction; release it with actor/time/reason. API rechecks active restrictions during submission, confirmation and allocation; SQL alone does not block those operations.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.documents IS 'What: Attachment metadata, parent record, private storage key and scan/visibility status. Why: Links files to business records without storing file binaries in database rows. How: Attach to exactly one shipper, booking or journey. Store bytes in private object storage; API validates type/size, scans files and authorizes downloads. Record each additional file separately.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.notifications IS 'What: One recipient''s in-app message, optional booking/journey link and read time. Why: Supports personal unread counts without sharing read state between users. How: Insert one row per recipient; set readon when read. API checks current authorization before exposing messages or opening their linked records. This table is not an email delivery queue.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.user_tokens IS 'What: Hashes of refresh, password-reset and verification tokens with lifecycle timestamps. Why: Supports revocable credentials without retaining plaintext token secrets. How: API issues tokens, stores only secure hashes, checks expiry/revocation and atomically consumes or rotates them. Purpose separates token uses; expiry is checked by the API.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kodiak.audit_logs IS 'What: Append-only change metadata: entity, action, actor, request and changed field names. Why: Helps investigate who changed a record and correlate changes with API requests. How: Triggers create entries automatically. No full before/after values are retained, so this cannot restore deleted records or reconstruct every previous value. entityid is a generic UUID, not a foreign key to every business table.';
COMMENT ON VIEW kodiak.journey_summary IS 'What: Query combining journeys, booking parties, container number, latest effective event and open-hold flag. Why: Gives dashboards and tracking lists a consistent current-state projection. How: Query the view with API authorization filters. It excludes replaced events and selects latest by occurredon, then createdon and ID. lastrecordedon is the entry time of that selected event, not necessarily the latest write anywhere in the journey. endedon/outcome remain the authoritative closure fields.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION kodiak.validate_user_scope() IS 'What: Trigger function that checks role-to-organization consistency. Why: Prevents a shipper login pointing to a consignee organization or an externally scoped admin. How: users_scope calls it before every user insert/update. It validates the row, not the caller''s permission to create accounts.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION kodiak.stamp_audit_columns() IS 'What: Trigger function that stamps actor IDs and database entry times. Why: Standardizes audit fields and preserves original creation metadata. How: API sets transaction-local kodiak.actor_id; the trigger reads it, stamps updates and preserves created fields on update. Missing actor context produces null actor IDs for bootstrap/system/anonymous operations.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION kodiak.log_change() IS 'What: Trigger function that inserts an audit_logs row after a successful row change. Why: Records changes consistently across business tables in the same transaction. How: Trigger reads kodiak.request_id and kodiak.change_reason when supplied. UPDATE entries list changed field names excluding updatedby/updatedon; INSERT/DELETE entries use an empty list. Audit rows are excluded from recursive logging.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION kodiak.reject_history_mutation() IS 'What: Trigger function that rejects UPDATE and DELETE on history tables. Why: Preserves review, tracking and audit evidence. How: preserve_history invokes it; add new review/correction records instead. Runtime permissions must additionally prohibit TRUNCATE and trigger disabling.';
COMMENT ON TRIGGER users_scope ON kodiak.users IS 'What: BEFORE INSERT/UPDATE trigger on users. Why: Applies the organization/role consistency check automatically. How: Calls validate_user_scope for each affected user row.';
COMMENT ON TRIGGER stamp_audit ON kodiak.countries IS 'What: BEFORE INSERT/UPDATE trigger on every table. Why: Ensures all 26 tables use the same four audit fields. How: Calls stamp_audit_columns once per row; new rows initialize both created and updated metadata.';
COMMENT ON TRIGGER stamp_audit ON kodiak.states IS 'What: BEFORE INSERT/UPDATE trigger on every table. Why: Ensures all 26 tables use the same four audit fields. How: Calls stamp_audit_columns once per row; new rows initialize both created and updated metadata.';
COMMENT ON TRIGGER stamp_audit ON kodiak.ports IS 'What: BEFORE INSERT/UPDATE trigger on every table. Why: Ensures all 26 tables use the same four audit fields. How: Calls stamp_audit_columns once per row; new rows initialize both created and updated metadata.';
COMMENT ON TRIGGER stamp_audit ON kodiak.consignees IS 'What: BEFORE INSERT/UPDATE trigger on every table. Why: Ensures all 26 tables use the same four audit fields. How: Calls stamp_audit_columns once per row; new rows initialize both created and updated metadata.';
COMMENT ON TRIGGER stamp_audit ON kodiak.shippers IS 'What: BEFORE INSERT/UPDATE trigger on every table. Why: Ensures all 26 tables use the same four audit fields. How: Calls stamp_audit_columns once per row; new rows initialize both created and updated metadata.';
